@meta
id: 09-01
title: An operator needs somewhere to go
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 14
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click the word, press Backspace twice
open: refunds.md

@brief
Chapter 8 gave you operators: `d`, `c`, `y`. Chapter 5 to 7 gave you
motions: `w`, `$`, `G`, `}` and the rest. Vim's whole grammar is that the
two halves clip together. An operator on its own does nothing; it waits.
Give it a motion and it applies itself to exactly the ground that motion
would have covered.

`dd` was a special case — the operator doubled means "this line". The
general case is an operator plus any motion at all, and there are a great
many motions.

This paragraph has a word typed twice. Delete the second one, leaving the
line reading normally, and save.

@keys
| `d` | delete — waits for a motion |
| `w` | forward to the start of the next word |
| `dw` | delete from the cursor up to the start of the next word |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the first character of the duplicated word. Word
motions are still the cheapest way down a line.
--
With the cursor on the word, `dw` deletes it — and the space after it,
because that space is ground the `w` motion covers.
--
Two `j` presses down, four `w` presses along, then `dw`. `4w` beats
pressing `w` four times.

@solution
    jj 4w dw :wq<Enter>

Ten keystrokes, and only two of them were the edit.

The important part is that `dw` is not a command you memorised. It is `d`
and `w` put together, and the same `d` takes every other motion you know.
`d$` deletes to the end of the line. `dG` deletes to the end of the file.
`d3w` deletes three words. You learned `w` in chapter 5 and it just became
a delete, a change and a yank without anyone teaching you three more keys.

That is the deal vim offers: learn ten operators and twenty motions and
you have two hundred edits, none of which you had to memorise separately.

Note what `dw` took: from the cursor up to, but not including, the first
character of the next word. The space came with it. That is not a rule
about `d`, it is a rule about `w` — `w` lands on the next word's start, so
everything before that point is what got deleted.

